# Lidar Plant Detection 
# Author : Shivali Munshi 
# Pennsylvania State University

Real-time plant stand classifier running on a Raspberry Pi 4, written in C.
Reads an 8×8 LiDAR distance grid and classifies each plant slot as Empty, Single, or Double at required speed.

 #What it does :

Empty = No plant in slot   =   Red 
Single = One green plant   =   Green 
Double = Green + yellow plant  =  Blue 

The classifier feeds directly into the robot's navigation and actuation systems. On a confirmed double detection, the system signals the camera thread to identify the yellow plant for removal by the robotic arm.

# Hardware

- Raspberry Pi 4
- DFRobot 8×8 Matrix 64-Pixel ToF LiDAR Sensor (VL53L5CX-based, USB/serial)
- RGB LEDs on GPIO 14 (blue), 15 (green), 10 (red)


## How it works

Serial (/dev/ttyACM0)
        |
        v
 [Thread 1 — Receiving]
  Reads 8x8 distance frame every 10ms
  Applies 4-frame temporal averaging filter
        |
        v (frame queue / mutex)
        |
 [Thread 2 — Controller]
  Applies distance threshold to binary mask
  Flood-fill blob counting on masked pixels
  0 blobs → Empty
  1 blob  → Single
  2 blobs → Double
  Sends LED command via FIFO queue
        |
        v (LED command queue / mutex)
        |
 [Thread 3 — LED]
  Writes GPIO pins via libgpiod v2


Terminal output every frame:

=== DISTANCE GRID (mm) ===
y0:   38   41   55  4000  4000  4000  4000  4000
y1:   52   54  4000  4000  4000  4000  4000  4000
...

=== BINARY GRID (1=plant 0=bg) ===
y0:    1    1    1    0    0    0    0    0
y1:    1    1    0    0    0    0    0    0
...

=== CLASSIFICATION ===
Blobs: 2  ->  DOUBLE (LED: BLUE)
Threshold: 216 mm


GTK window shows the 8×8 grid as a black/white bitmap (black = plant, white = background).



## Build

sudo apt-get install libgtk-4-dev libgpiod-dev

gcc lidar6.c draw_bitmap_multiwindow.c wait_key.c \
    $(pkg-config --cflags --libs gtk4) \
    -Wall -Wno-deprecated-declarations -g \
    -o lidar6 -lm -lpthread -lgpiod


## Run this : 
sudo chmod a+rw /dev/gpiochip0
DISPLAY=:0 ./lidar_final


On first launch: aim sensor at an empty slot and press Enter to calibrate the baseline distance threshold automatically.


## Calibration

The program measures background distance at startup and sets:

threshold = baseline_average - 150mm

Anything closer than the threshold = plant (black). Everything else = background (white). No hardcoded distance needed — adapts to each deployment location.

To tune sensitivity, adjust `BASELINE_MARGIN` in `lidar_final.c`:
- Lower value → stricter (fewer false positives, may miss distant plants)
- Higher value → more sensitive (catches more, may pick up ground/noise)

